# Weights of regions and the unconditional Lemma 2.3 (Chazelle §2.2–2.3)

## The paper's definitions (p. 494, p. 496)

* "We define the *weight* of a region as `0` if the region is empty, or else as the maximum
  number of nonnull length edges in any of its arcs. … an arc never contains any chord."
* A submap is *γ-granular* if (i) every node of its tree has weight at most `γ` and
  (ii) contracting any edge incident upon at least one node of degree less than `3` produces a
  new node whose weight exceeds `γ`; *γ-semigranular* if only (i) holds; *conformal* if its
  tree has node degree at most `4` (p. 495).
* **Lemma 2.3.** *Any γ-granular conformal submap … has `O(n/γ + 1)` regions and each region is
  bounded by `O(γ)` edges.*

## The weights in the box model

The edges of `∂C` bounding a region are the pieces of the sides of the chain edges facing it
(`Chazelle.Chain.Arcs`).  We define two weights of a region `R` of a submap `S`:

* `totalWeight S R` — the number of pieces facing `R` (the total number of incidences of `R`
  with `∂C`);
* `arcWeight S R` — **the paper's weight**: the maximum, over the *arcs* of `R`, of the number of
  edges of `∂C` (pairs `(edge, side)`) met by the arc.  An arc is a class of the equivalence
  generated by `ArcLink`: two pieces are linked when they are consecutive along `∂C`
  (`ArcNext`: across a cut parameter on the same side of an edge, across a chain vertex on the
  same side, or around one of the two endpoints of the chain to the other side) and face the
  same region.  Consecutive pieces facing the same region are not separated by a chord of `S`
  (two pieces separated by a chord face the two different regions that the chord separates), so
  these classes are the maximal pieces of `∂C` containing no chord endpoint, i.e. the paper's
  arcs; the paper's zero-length arcs and null chords have no Euclidean counterpart (the
  Euclidean model has no null chords).  Within a side of an edge this identification is a
  theorem (`arcLink_next_iff`: consecutive pieces across a cut parameter are linked iff no chord
  of `S` arrives there from that side); at chain vertices and around the two endpoints of the
  chain it is documentation (a chord in the sector at the vertex separates the two pieces, by
  `chord_separates` and the fact that two distinct chords give distinct edges of the dual
  tree; at the topmost vertex the slit of the box model lies in one of the two sectors, and
  whether the two pieces on either side of it are linked is decided by the regions they face).
  The only fact about `arcWeight` used for Lemma 2.3 is `arcWeight_le_totalWeight`.

Main results:

* `arcWeight_le_totalWeight`: `arcWeight S R ≤ totalWeight S R` (an arc is a set of pieces
  facing `R`, and it meets each edge side in at least one of its pieces);
* `sum_totalWeight_le` (**`hsum`**): the total weight of any set of distinct regions is at most
  `2 m + 4 |S|` — each piece faces exactly one region (`ncard_incidences_le`);
* `totalWeight_merge` (**`hmerge`**): removing a chord `c ∈ S` merges its two regions `U, V` into
  the region `W` of `S \ {c}`, and `totalWeight (S \ {c}) W ≤ totalWeight S U + totalWeight S V`
  (every piece of `S \ {c}` facing `W` contains a piece of `S` with the same start, which faces
  `U` or `V`, `eq_or_eq_of_subset_merge`); consequently `arcWeight_merge_le`;
* **Lemma 2.3, region count, unconditional** (`lemma_2_3`): for a conformal γ-granular submap
  `S ⊆ C.chords` (with the paper's weight `arcWeight`),
  `|S| (γ + 1) ≤ 12 (2 m + 4 |S|)`; hence (`lemma_2_3_regions`) for `γ ≥ 94` the number of
  regions (`= |S| + 1`) satisfies `(#regions - 1) γ ≤ 48 m`, i.e.
  `#regions ≤ 48 m / γ + 1` (`lemma_2_3_regions_div`), and for every `γ`,
  `(#regions - 1) γ ≤ 188 (m + 1)` (`lemma_2_3_regions_all`).  The same holds for granularity
  measured with `totalWeight` (`lemma_2_3_totalWeight`) or with any weight bounded by it
  (`ncard_mul_le_of_contraction`).  Only condition (ii) of granularity and conformality are
  used.
* Second half of Lemma 2.3, arithmetic part (`sum_arcEdges_le`): in a γ-semigranular submap,
  `k` arcs of a region carry at most `k γ` edges of `∂C`.

**On `hmerge` for the maximal-arc weight.**  The docstring of
`Chazelle.Chain.ncard_mul_le_of_granular_merge` asserts `weight W ≤ weight U + weight V` for
the paper's weight, "every arc of `W` is an arc of `U`, an arc of `V`, or two glued".  This is
**false** in general: if `V` is a leaf region bounded by `c` and a single arc `α`, the arc of
`W` through `c`'s endpoints is `A₁ α A₂` where `A₁, A₂` are the *two* arcs of `U` adjacent to
`c`, so `weight W` can be close to `2 weight U + weight V`.  The count does not need it: the
proof goes through `totalWeight`, which *is* subadditive, and `arcWeight ≤ totalWeight`.  (The
weights of `S` and of `S \ {c}` are different functions, so the result is stated in that form
and fed to `ncard_mul_le_of_granular` rather than to `ncard_mul_le_of_granular_merge`.)

**Not proved here** (open): the second half of Lemma 2.3 ("each region is bounded by `O(γ)`
edges") beyond its arithmetic part — it needs a bound on the number of arcs of a region by its
dual degree (Lemma 2.2's alternation of arcs and exit chords).  Note that `totalWeight` is not
`O(γ)` in general (a side of an edge is cut into many pieces by chords arriving from the other
side), so the bound must be stated with `arcEdges`.
-/

